## Why This Is Asked
Tests data-structure augmentation in a Trie to support prefix queries and k-th item retrieval with weights, mirroring autocomplete and ranking systems.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with per-subtree weights
- Prefix sum queries in O(length of prefix)
- K-th word selection within a prefix using cumulative weights
- Handling duplicates and deletions robustly

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next=new Map(); this.subWeight=0; this.endWeight=0; this.isWord=false; } }
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word,w){ let node=this.root; node.subWeight+=w; for(let ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.subWeight+=w; } node.isWord=true; node.endWeight+=w; }
  delete(word,w){ this.insert(word,-w); }
  sumPrefix(prefix){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node=node.next.get(ch); } return node.subWeight; }
  kthWord(prefix,k){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return null; node=node.next.get(ch); }
    function dfs(n, path){ if(n.isWord && n.endWeight>0){ if(--k===0) return path; }
      for(let c of [...n.next.keys()].sort()){ let child=n.next.get(c); if(child.subWeight>=k){ const res=dfs(child, path+c); if(res) return res; } else { k-=child.subWeight; } }
      return null; }
    return dfs(node, prefix);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle Unicode alphabets or memory limits?
- How would you support tie-breaking for equal weights?
- How would you optimize for bulk updates and high deletion rates?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem validates DP mastery (two implementations) and handling of substring lookups with a dictionary. It stresses practical constraints and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Bottom-up DP, Memoization, Time: O(N * maxLen)
- Hash-map lookups for dictionary scores
- Edge cases: no decomposition, empty input, overlapping choices

## Code Example
```javascript
// Bottom-up
function maxScore(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const n = s.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(NEG);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(i, maxLen); len++) {
      const substr = s.slice(i - len, i);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr) && dp[i - len] !== NEG) {
        dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[i - len] + dict[substr]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === NEG ? -1 : dp[n];
}

// Top-down
function maxScoreTopDown(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const memo = new Map();
  function solve(i) {
    if (i === s.length) return 0;
    if (memo.has(i)) return memo.get(i);
    let best = -Infinity;
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(maxLen, s.length - i); len++) {
      const substr = s.substring(i, i + len);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr)) {
        const val = dict[substr] + solve(i + len);
        if (val > best) best = val;
      }
    }
    memo.set(i, best);
    return best;
  }
  const res = solve(0);
  return res === -Infinity ? -1 : res;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt it if dictionary lookups are expensive and you need pruning?
- How would you extend to handle negative scores or required minimum total?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of in-place sorting algorithms, particularly the challenging problem of merging sorted subarrays without auxiliary space. It distinguishes candidates who understand the theoretical difference between standard merge sort (O(n) space) and true in-place variants, and reveals familiarity with classical algorithms like the Ford-Johnson merge sort or rotation-based merging.

## Key Concepts

- **In-place merge**: Merging two sorted subarrays within the original array without allocating a separate buffer
- **Stability**: Preserving the relative order of equal elements, critical for multi-key sorting
- **Bottom-up merge sort**: Iterative approach that starts with singleton runs and doubles the merge width each pass
- **Rotation-based merging**: Using cyclic block swaps to interleave elements from two sorted segments
- **Time/space complexities**: O(n log n) average time, O(n²) worst-case due to rotation cost; O(1) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function rotate(arr, l, m, r) {
  // Rotates arr[l..m-1] with arr[m..r-1] in-place
  // Uses block-swap: hold arr[l], shift left block right by one, repeat cyclically
}

function inPlaceMerge(arr, l, m, r) {
  // Merge sorted runs [l, m) and [m, r) in-place
  // Find the smaller run, rotate it into position
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr) {
  // Bottom-up: start with width=1, double each iteration
  for (let w = 1; w < arr.length; w *= 2) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += 2 * w) {
      inPlaceMerge(arr, i, Math.min(i + w, arr.length), Math.min(i + 2 * w, arr.length));
    }
  }
}
```

**In-place example**: arr = [1, 3, 5 | 2, 4, 6], merging runs [1,3,5] and [2,4,6] rotates to [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].

## Follow-up Questions

- How does rotation cost O(n) per merge affect constants and worst-case performance?
- How would you adapt this for linked data structures?
- What optimizations would improve practical performance despite theoretical worst-case?
- How does this compare to in-place quicksort or heap sort?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a clean DP formulation for segmentation with per-placement costs and wildcards. It also probes backtracking and memoization versus tabulation choices under a dictionary constraint.

## Key Concepts
- DP on string positions with a dictionary
- Memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Backtracking reconstruction of segmentation
- Handling wildcards and per-placement costs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCost(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  const choice = new Array(n).fill(null);

  function costAt(i, w) {
    let cost = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < w.length; j++) {
      if (s[i+j] !== '?' && s[i+j] !== w[j]) {
        cost++;
      }
    }
    return cost;
  }

  function dp(i) {
    if (i === n) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== null) return memo[i];

    let best = Infinity;
    let bestWord = null;

    for (const w of dict) {
      if (i + w.length <= n) {
        const c = costAt(i, w) + dp(i + w.length);
        if (c < best) {
          best = c;
          bestWord = w;
        }
      }
    }

    memo[i] = best;
    choice[i] = bestWord;
    return best;
  }

  const minCost = dp(0);
  if (minCost === Infinity) return -1;

  // Reconstruct segmentation
  const words = [];
  let i = 0;
  while (i < n) {
    words.push(choice[i]);
    i += choice[i].length;
  }

  return { cost: minCost, words };
}
```

## Complexity
Time: O(n × sum|w|) where sum|w| is total length of all dictionary words
Space: O(n) for memoization and choice arrays

